WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Last time, one node halved its exit rate constant.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.200
With entry fixed, it held twice as many cells.

3
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.720
But what if every node slowed its exit at once?

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.020
Why does the way out set the stay?

5
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.300
A lymphocyte in the spleen has no scheduled departure.

6
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.620
The fitted model treats leaving as chance.

7
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.140
So the same fraction leaves each minute, not the same number.

8
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.740
In the rat spleen, it is 0.7 percent a minute.

9
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.240
That holds for a thousand cells or a billion.

10
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:41.233
So how fast cells arrive has no effect on how long each stays.

11
00:00:42.793 --> 00:00:43.793
Try made-up entry.

12
00:00:45.093 --> 00:00:49.373
A thousand naive CD4 T cells enter one mouse node each hour.

13
00:00:50.573 --> 00:00:52.093
So do a thousand CD8.

14
00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:56.803
In mice, CD4 cells cross a node in about 12 hours.

15
00:00:58.223 --> 00:01:00.023
CD8 cells take about 21.

16
00:01:01.363 --> 00:01:05.783
So the node holds about 1.7 times as many CD8 cells.

17
00:01:07.143 --> 00:01:09.843
Entry was equal, so the difference is the stay alone.

18
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:12.487
Here is the whole rule.

19
00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:15.707
Exit sets how long each cell stays.

20
00:01:17.087 --> 00:01:18.367
Entry sets how many arrive.

21
00:01:19.707 --> 00:01:23.347
And entry into a node rises and falls with the cells in blood.

22
00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:29.017
Return to the rat model, kept to blood, spleen and the nodes under the skin.

23
00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:32.497
Blood holds about 4 percent of these cells.

24
00:01:33.777 --> 00:01:37.257
Now halve the exit rate constant of all those nodes at once.

25
00:01:38.537 --> 00:01:40.177
Each cell stays twice as long.

26
00:01:41.437 --> 00:01:43.057
Do the nodes hold twice as many?

27
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.110
No. The pool is closed.

28
00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:57.010
Blood's share falls from about 4 percent to about 2.5.

29
00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.830
Entry into the nodes falls by the same factor.

30
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:05.890
Their share rises only from about 63 percent to 77.

31
00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.950
That is 1.23 times, not 2.

32
00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:13.143
A single draining node is different.

33
00:02:14.363 --> 00:02:18.943
It is one among hundreds, so slowing its exit barely changes the blood count.

34
00:02:20.363 --> 00:02:21.963
Its entry keeps its old rate.

35
00:02:23.203 --> 00:02:25.143
So halving exit doubles what it holds.

36
00:02:26.217 --> 00:02:27.877
Why hold cells there at all?

37
00:02:29.037 --> 00:02:31.397
This node drains a site of immune challenge.

38
00:02:32.577 --> 00:02:37.677
The longer stay gives the rare cells that recognise the antigen more time to find

39
00:02:37.677 --> 00:02:41.557
it. The shutdown is transient, and exit resumes.

40
00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:46.087
So a node sets the stay, but blood supplies the cells.

41
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:49.567
One node can draw on the whole circulation.

42
00:02:50.947 --> 00:02:53.567
When every node draws at once, blood runs short.

43
00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:57.407
The gain is then far smaller than the longer stay.
